                                                        UA(LOCAL)NAME     ua - the UNaXcess multi-user conferencing systemSYNOPSIS     ua [``_ˆu_ˆs_ˆe_ˆr_ˆn_ˆa_ˆm_ˆe'']DESCRIPTION     The UNaXcess conferencing system is a multi-user BBS-style     message system.  Currently it supports any number of user-     created conferences on any subjects, and a file up/download     library with any number of branches.  UNaXcess may be run     from the shell or as a UNIX(R) login.  As a login shell, it     prompts for a user name and a password (if applicable).     There are usually some special user names, such as _ˆn_ˆe_ˆw and     _ˆG_ˆU_ˆE_ˆS_ˆT.  The user name _ˆo_ˆf_ˆf exits to the login: prompt.  From     the shell, a user may specify his user name in quotes (to     protect spaces and special characters from the shell); or,     if hir UNaXcess user name is the same as hir UNIX login     name, s/he will be logged in to UNaXcess directly.  Once     logged in, the user is shown the latest bulletins, then any     new messages and new or deleted conferences are shown, fol-     lowed by the main command prompt.  This prompt shows the     current conference and the time remaining (if time limits     are imposed), followed by the prompt                        Command (? for help):     At this point, any of the UNaXcess commands may be entered;     see the COMMANDS section of this manual for more details.NEW USERS     The user name _ˆn_ˆe_ˆw is most often used to register new users.     A new user will be shown the new user bulletin (separate     from the standard bulletins), and will be asked if s/he     still wants to become a user.  If so, s/he will be asked a     series of questions for registration purposes:  hir full     name, preferred password, full address, and phone number.     If s/he has a standard UNIX login, s/he will be asked to     supply it; this simplifies the sysop's checking for poten-     tial system-level access (see ACCESS MODES below).  Depend-     ing on the system, the new user will either be logged out or     permitted to use the system.COMMANDS     UNaXcess has three menus:  the Main Menu, the Message Base     Menu, and the Administration Menu.Eachmenuhastwo question     mark (?) once displays the short help message, pressing it a     second time displays a full help file.1. The Main Menu     The following commands are available on the Main Menu:                                                                1UA(LOCAL)          A - Enter the Administration Menu.  See the               Administration Menu below for details.          B - Redisplay the new bulletins.  Between each               bulletin, the user is asked whether s/he               wishes to Continue reading bulletins or Stop.          C - For users with System access (see ACCESS LEV-               ELS below), this command starts up a shell.               Unfortunately, this command doesn't work so               well from a UNaXcess login "shell"; the               user's UNIX permissions are those of the UNIX               login.          D - Change the default conference.  Each user has               a ``default'' conference which s/he automati-               cally joins when s/he logs in.  For new               users, this is set to the _ˆg_ˆe_ˆn_ˆe_ˆr_ˆa_ˆl conference,               but the D command allows a user to change               this.          F - Enter the File UDL area.  Users with File UDL               access (see ACCESS MODES below) can upload               and download files; other users may only look               at the UDL library.  See THE FILE UDL AREA               below for more information.          G - Exit UNaXcess.  Shell users will be returned               to thatever they were doing before they               entered UNaXcess; others will be returned to               the login: prompt.               If the Main Menu is entered from the Message               Loop (see the Message Base Menu), the G com-               mand returns the user to the Message Loop.          M - Enter the Message Base Menu.  See below for               details.          W - Show the UNaXcess user log.  This shows the               user name and access level of each user;               Fairwitnesses (see THE FAIRWITNESS below) can               also see the address and phone number for               validation purposes.2. The Message Base Menu     The primary message base commands are contained on this     menu.  The commands are:          D - Change the default conference.  Each user has               a ``default'' conference which s/he automati-               cally joins when s/he logs in.  For new2                                                        UA(LOCAL)               users, this is set to the _ˆg_ˆe_ˆn_ˆe_ˆr_ˆa_ˆl conference,               but the D command allows a user to change               this.          E - Enter a message.  The user is prompted for the               recipient, the message's subject, and the               conference to which the message should be               posted.  (The current conference is the               default.)  S/he may also be asked whether the               message is to be private or not; the default               is no.  Private messages may not be enabled               on all UNaXcess systems, and if they are,               they may only be sent to existing users.  The               user is then placed in the message editor,               which automatically word-wraps the message.               Any of CONTROL-D (the standard UNIX end-of-               message indicator), CONTROL-Z (the end-of-               file indicator for some microcomputer sys-               tems), or ESCAPE will end the message and               display the Send prompt.  At this point the               user may choose to continue the message, edit               it, list it, send it, or abort it.  S/he is               asked for confirmation if s/he chooses to               abort the message.          G - Exit UNaXcess.  Shell users will be returned               to thatever they were doing before they               entered UNaXcess; others will be returned to               the login: prompt.               If the Main Menu is entered from the Message               Loop (see the Message Base Menu), the G com-               mand returns the user to the Message Loop.          I - Display the index of conferences.  For each               conference, the name, number of messages, and               number of messages read by the current user               are shown; if the conference is restricted,               this fact is noted.  If the user is unsub-               scribed from the conference, this will also               be shown.          J - Join a different conference, or create a new               conference.  The user is prompted for the               conference to join; if it doesn't exist, s/he               is asked if s/he wants to create it.  If so,               s/he is placed in the Message Editor to com-               pose a message describing the conference's               purpose.               If the conference is restricted and the user               is not a member, s/he will be informed of the                                                                3UA(LOCAL)               fact and returned to the Message Base Menu.               If the user is unsubscribed, s/he will be               asked if s/he wishes to resubscribe to the               conference.  If not, s/he will be returned to               the Message Base Menu.  See the U command for               more details.               A conference name can be up to at least 14               characters in length; on Berkeley UNIX sys-               tems, it may be up to 32 characters in               length.  It may not contain colons or               slashes; these will be converted to dots.               Case is not significant in a conference name.          K - Kill a message.  The user must be the owner or               recipient of the message, or a Fairwitness,               to kill a message.  S/he will be prompted for               the message number to kill.  Only messages in               the current conference can be killed.          N - Read all new messages in all conferences.               Unsubscribed conferences and restricted               conferences of which the user is not a member               are skipped silently.  This command works               like the R command in the Read Commands Menu               in the New mode (see below) within each               conference; between conferences the user is               asked whether s/he wishes to go to the Next               conference, Unsubscribe from the current               conference (see below) or Stop.          R - Enter the Read Commands Menu.  See below for               more details.          U - Unsubscribe from a conference.  New messages               in the conference will not be noted at login,               and the conference will be skipped during an               N command; the user must Join the conference               (J command) in order to resubscribe.          X - Return to the Main Menu.          ? - This shows the help menu, similarly to the               Main Menu's help command.3. The Read Commands menu     This menu contains the read commands, all of which work in     the same basic way.  Each command asks whether the user     wishes to read Forward (F), Reverse (R), an Individual mes-     sage (I), or all New messages (N).  If the user chooses F or     R, s/he will be asked for the first and last messages to4                                                        UA(LOCAL)     read; the defaults are message 1 and the last message in the     conference.  If s/he chooses I, s/he will be asked for the     message number to read.  If s/he chooses the N command, s/he     will be shown all messages posted since s/he last read the     messages in that conference in ascending (chronological)     order.  Only the messages in the current conference can be     read.  The commands are:          G - Exit UNaXcess.  Shell users will be returned               to thatever they were doing before they               entered UNaXcess; others will be returned to               the login: prompt.               If the Main Menu is entered from the Message               Loop (see the Message Base Menu), the G com-               mand returns the user to the Message Loop.          N - Read all new messages.  This command is ident-               ical to the N command of the Message Base               Menu and is placed here for the user's con-               venience.          Q - Quick scan of messages.  Only the subjects of               messages are shown.          R - Read messages.  The full text of each message               is shown.          S - Scan messages.  Only the headers of messages               are shown.          X - Return to the Message Base Menu.     After each message read by the N or R commands, the user is     placed in the Read Loop.  From this mini-menu, the following     commands are available:          C - Continue to the next message.          S - Stop reading messages; from the R command, the               user is returned to the Read Menu; from the N               command the user is sent to the Next               Conference/Stop prompt.  (See the N command               above.)          K - Kill the current message.  Only the sender or               recipient of a message, or a Fairwitness, may               Kill a message.          U - Unsubscribe from the current conference.  From               the N command, the user is sent to the Next               Conference/Stop prompt; from the R command,                                                                5UA(LOCAL)               s/he is returned to the Read Commands Menu.          R - Reply to the current message.  The user is               asked for the conference to which the reply               should be directed (the default is the               conference containing the message).  On some               UNaXcess systems, the user will be asked               whether the message is private or not; the               default is the privacy status of the original               message.          E - Enter a new message.  This functions identi-               cally to the E command of the Message Base               Menu, save that the message will be placed by               default into the conference from which the E               subcommand is entered.          X - eXecute.  The user will be placed in the Main               Menu, with a changed prompt:                    Command (? for help, G to return):               The G command, rather than exiting UNaXcess,               will return the user to the Read Loop.4. The Administration Menu     The Administration Menu contains commands useful for UNaX-     cess system administration.  Some of the commands are dupli-     cated on the Main Menu, and some are usable by ordinary     users.  The commands are:          A - Alter a user.  If the current user is a               Fairwitness, see the UNaXcess Administrator's               Manual for details on usage.               Ordinary users may only alter themselves, and               only certain parameters:  their password,               line length, or screen size.  The subcommands               available to ordinary users are:                    P - Change the user's password.                         The user is prompted to enter                         a new password of between 3                         and 8 characters.                    S - Change the line Size (line                         length).  The user is prompted                         for a new line length of                         between 40 and 132 characters.                         The default line length is 80,                         suitable for use on a standard                         terminal.6                                                        UA(LOCAL)                    L - Change the Lines per screen.                         The user is prompted for a new                         screen size of between 0 and                         66.  A value of 0 disables all                         display paging and is useful                         on hardcopy terminals such as                         the TI "Silent 700"(TM).ACCESS LEVELS     There are 5 standard access levels in UNaXcess.  They are:          Guest - The Guest access level permits a user to               read messages in any non-restricted confer-               ence; however, the user may not use the C               command, upload or download files, or enter               messages except in the _ˆg_ˆu_ˆe_ˆs_ˆt conference, if               it exists.          Messages - The Messages access level permits a               user to read or enter messages in non-               restricted conferences.  S/he may not use the               C command or upload or download files.          File UDL - The File UDL access level allows a user               to upload or download files.  The C command               is still off limits, however.          System - The System access level allows a user too               use the C command to get a shell.  The shell               will have the permissions of the user who ran               UNaXcess; from a shell prompt, it will be               that of the currently-logged-in user, but               from a UNaXcess login "shell" it will be that               of the UNIX(R) user owning that login.          Fairwitness - A Fairwitness is a special user, as               described previously.  A Fairwitness may read               or kill any message in any conference,               including restricted conferences.  However, a               Fairwitness must be a member of a restricted               conference in order to change the membership               list for that conference.  S/he may also               create and post messages to Read-Only confer-               ences on some systems;  this permits the               creation of truly moderated forums.          Sysop - The sysop is a very special user indeed;               no restrictions whatsoever apply to hir.               S/he is the only user permitted to restrict               or de-restrict a conference, or to promote or               demote Fairwitnesses.                                                                7UA(LOCAL)THE FAIRWITNESS     It should be obvious by now that the Fairwitness is not     intended to be simply an assistant sysop.  S/he is, in fact,     intended to be a discussion leader; s/he is in charge of a     conference or a group of conferences, and has a responsibil-     ity to stimulate discussion in those conferences.  The means     of doing this are to condense and recap previously posted     messages, to point out possibly-obscure relationships     between the current topic and other topics, and to ask ques-     tions designed to stimulate discussion.  A good Fairwitness     can make or break a conference.  The original idea of a     Fairwitness came from the CommuniTree Group, a non-profit     organization in California.  This group pioneered the con-     ferencing BBS system via their "Conference Tree" program.     They lifted the Fairwitness from the novel _ˆS_ˆT_ˆR_ˆA_ˆN_ˆG_ˆE_ˆR _ˆI_ˆN _ˆA     _ˆS_ˆT_ˆR_ˆA_ˆN_ˆG_ˆE _ˆL_ˆA_ˆN_ˆD by Robert A. Heinlein -- but with some major     differences.  The CommuniTree Group's guidelines for a     Fairwitness are preserved intact in UNaXcess.  Their intent     was the evolution of a new kind of BBS system:  a system     designed to stimulate creative and critical thinking, rather     than a literal electronic "bulletin board", classified-ad,     or "dial-your-match" kind of system.  As a former Fairwit-     ness of the now-defunct Conference Tree #1085, the Great     North CommuniTree, I stand by their aims, and hope that     UNaXcess will help to fulfill them.THE FILE UDL AREA     The File UDL area is arranged as a "library" consisting of     multiple "branches".  Each "branch" (hopefully) contains     files to be downloaded, and has an upload area.  The     branches are usually set up by subject or by computer.  On     entry to the File UDL area, the user is shown a list of     branches and is prompted to select a branch.  After select-     ing one, s/he enters the File UDL Menu, consisting of the     following commands:          U - Upload a file to the current branch.  The user               is asked for a file name, a description, and               an upload method.          D - Download a file from the current branch.  The               user is asked for a file name and a download               method.          L - Display a list of the files in the current               branch.          G - Download a list of the files in the current               branch.  Any user may execute this function,               regardless of access level.  The user is               asked for the download method desired.8                                                        UA(LOCAL)          E - Redisplay the list of File UDL branches.     UNaXcess supports three file transfer protocols:          Xmodem - The "standard" file transfer method, also               known as "MODEM7" or "Ward Christensen" pro-               tocol.          Kermit - The micro-to-mainframe file transfer               method developed at Columbia University.  The               Kermit program is in the public domain, and               is available from Columbia University               directly and from many BBSes across the coun-               try.          ASCII - As a lowest common denominator, straight               ASCII transfer is available.  No error check-               ing is performed, and it is not advised to               send or receive binary data.  An ASCII upload               is terminated with a CONTROL-D.     Not all transfer methods may be available on all systems     (although they should be; public-domain implementations of     Xmodem and Kermit are freely available).  The ASCII method     is guaranteed to be available.  Uploaded files are not     automatically placed in the download area of a branch; a     Fairwitness must move them there.  This makes possible     checking for "trojan horse" programs.RESTRICTED CONFERENCES     The sysop may choose to restrict a conference.  This limits     access to a conference to the sysop, all Fairwitnesses, and     any users listed as members of the conference, making possi-     ble special interest groups (SIGs), users' groups, etc.     Only the sysop may restrict or remove restrictions on a     conference.  While any Fairwitness may read, enter, or kill     messages within a restricted conference, s/he may only add     or delete members if s/he is listed as a member of the     conference hirself.  A user attempting to Join a restricted     conference will not be permitted to do so if s/he is not a     member.  Restricted conferences will automatically be     skipped by the N command of the Message Base Menu if the     user is not a member of the conference.ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS AND COPYRIGHTS     UNIX(R) is a registered trademark of AT&T.     TI(R) and "Sile